WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.135
Here is a fee function.

2
00:00:04.135 --> 00:00:07.193
You hand it an amount, and it hands back the fee.

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.847
A test passes in 200.00 and expects 3.00.

4
00:00:10.847 --> 00:00:13.621
At ten in the morning, it passes.

5
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:17.573
At five in the afternoon, the nightly build gets 4.00.

6
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.387
Same amount, same code.

7
00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:21.803
Where did the extra euro come from?

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.069
It reads the wall clock.

9
00:00:25.069 --> 00:00:28.753
After the 16:00 cut-off, it adds a late surcharge.

10
00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:33.091
It also reads a global rate table that can change at any time.

11
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.037
And every call writes a line to the audit log.

12
00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:38.195
The signature shows one input.

13
00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:40.894
The clock and the rates are hidden inputs.

14
00:00:40.894 --> 00:00:46.036
The log line is a side effect: something the call does besides return its value.

15
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.352
Now take them out.

16
00:00:47.352 --> 00:00:51.959
The time and the rates come in as values, and the caller writes the log.

17
00:00:51.959 --> 00:00:53.943
Every input is now a parameter.

18
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.305
At 15:59 it gives 3.00.

19
00:00:57.305 --> 00:00:59.373
At 16:00, 4.00.

20
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:02.696
Run it again tonight, or on eight threads.

21
00:01:02.696 --> 00:01:03.671
What comes out?

22
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.521
4.00, every time.

23
00:01:06.521 --> 00:01:09.468
The real clock can spin all it likes.

24
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.284
That is a pure function.

25
00:01:11.284 --> 00:01:13.989
Its result depends only on its arguments.

26
00:01:13.989 --> 00:01:16.760
And calling it changes nothing outside it.

27
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:20.367
Here is a smaller fee function.

28
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:22.544
20,000 cents in, 300 out.

29
00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:25.550
This line calls it twice and adds the answers.

30
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.549
Could you call it once, and use the answer twice?

31
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:30.469
Yes.

32
00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:35.913
Swap each call for its value: 300 plus 300 is 600.

33
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:39.292
Now a call that charges a card.

34
00:01:39.292 --> 00:01:43.611
Store the receipt in c, and put c in a list twice: one charge.

35
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.082
Now inline c.

36
00:01:45.082 --> 00:01:46.757
How many charges?

37
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:48.510
Two.

38
00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:52.663
The customer paid twice, and nothing in the code warned you.

39
00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:56.790
This swap has a name: referential transparency.

40
00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.557
Only pure calls allow it.

41
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:04.195
In Java and Kotlin, these two totals always agree, because fee is pure.

42
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.684
The rule: a pure function takes everything as parameters, and gives everything back as its return value.

43
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:17.204
Quick check: isExpired compares a card's expiry with YearMonth.now().

44
00:02:17.204 --> 00:02:17.588
Pure?

45
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:19.770
Not pure.

46
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.429
The clock is a hidden input again.
